<%# What a fanedit is, as against what it plays like: the film it was cut from, who cut it,
    what kind of cut it is, and where it was published. None of it is in TMDB or OMDB, so
    the form is the only way it gets filled in.

    Hidden rather than absent when the media select says anything else -- see
    fanedit_fields_controller.js for why, and for what opens it again. `hidden` is enough
    on its own: Bootstrap's reboot carries `[hidden] { display: none !important }`. %>
<div class="fanedit-fields" data-fanedit-fields-target="panel" <%= 'hidden' unless entry.media == 'fanedit' %>>
  <%= f.input :fanedit_type, collection: Entry::FANEDIT_TYPES,
        label: 'Kind of edit', include_blank: '— Not said —' %>
  <%# The name of the source material, not a link to it. The entry's own IMDb id or
      Letterboxd slug is what the card links this through, so a fanedit with either one
      gets the link without anybody pasting it twice. %>
  <%= f.input :original, label: 'Original',
        hint: 'What it was cut from. Linked to IMDb or Letterboxd on the card when the entry has an id for it.' %>
  <%= f.input :faneditor, label: 'Faneditor' %>
  <%# The editor's page for the cut -- fanedits.org, a forum thread, wherever it was
      published. Not where it plays from: that is the Source URL above, and the
      broken-source sweep only knows about that one. %>
  <%= f.input :fanedit_link, as: :url, label: 'Fanedit link',
        input_html: { placeholder: 'https://…' },
        hint: 'Where the edit was published. Not where it plays from.' %>
</div>
